WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.220
Now to growing concerns

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.350
about the deadly coronavirus
officially hitting the U.S.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.360
One person with coronavirus

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.883
in King County, Washington State has died.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.230
So it's been two years
since the first case

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.430
of Covid was detected in the U.S.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.880
And we've had a major scientific
breakthrough since then,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.990
vaccines, antivirals, probably
what's most significant

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.303
in the background is genomic sequencing.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.720
That's the work that's been
done by scientific labs

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.910
to figure out what mutations
people have of coronavirus

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.690
and what those mutations do
when they do infect a person.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.550
It's really to help adapt
vaccines really quickly

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.890
and prevent that strain
from doing a significant

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:48.883
amount of damage.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.550
I headed out to Seattle
where they had detected

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.760
the first case of Covid in the community

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.850
to ask the scientists
who've been on the frontline

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.380
of Covid detection and
mutation surveillance,

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.173
are we any better prepared?

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.260
Yeah, sorry, this question
makes me really mad.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.360
I'm hoping we've learned a lot of lessons.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.390
So we're in downtown Seattle.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.110
When I started thinking
through the question

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.070
of how do we assess whether or
not we're any better prepared

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.940
for the next pandemic?

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.820
All my reporting actually led here.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.380
And basically the reason
Seattle was so prepared

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.120
by the time coronavirus came
along is they had already

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.620
started a project called
the Seattle Flu Study.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.370
It started in 2018 and
by 2019 they were doing

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.670
this swab and send program where people

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.040
could take a nasal swab,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.190
which you probably had
never thought of doing

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.760
before the coronavirus pandemic,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.740
but people were doing it
here just to figure out

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.160
what kind of respiratory illnesses

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.130
were circulating in the community.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.250
Because they were in this position

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.410
and they were doing this work,

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.350
they found the first case
of community-acquired Covid

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.930
in the U.S.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.800
We're here to talk to the
scientists who came up

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.610
with that project and who
are hopefully gonna see that

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.110
through and perhaps expand it.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.937
So the Seattle Flu Study started in 2018.

00:02:12.937 --> 00:02:15.950
And at the time we called
this the Seattle Flu Study,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.510
mainly because when everybody
is sick in the winter,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.630
they think they have the
flu, even if they don't.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.600
And secondly, as a prototype
pandemic preparedness platform,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.520
everybody thought the next
pandemic would be a flu

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.470
or an influenza.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.740
So these are samples

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.310
from the Seattle coronavirus
assessment network.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.400
We're interested in what's
going on in the community

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.740
with people who are infected
with some respiratory pathogen,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.930
they're not sick enough
to go to the doctor.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.213
What do they have and can we find it?

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.638
So when somebody signs up
for the Seattle Flu Study,

00:02:47.638 --> 00:02:50.500
usually it's because they've woken up

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.880
with some sort of illness and the person

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.060
follows the instructions
to swab themselves

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.710
and put that swab safely in
a tube in a specimen bag.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:03.380
We test it for Covid and 20
something other pathogens.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.990
We found out about coronavirus
just like everybody else,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.030
through the news.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.890
We immediately saw that,
hey, if we just turn on

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.300
a test for coronavirus at the same time,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.540
we can be screening
for all of these things

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.510
to understand what coronavirus looks like

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.700
in the community right now.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.830
There was already a
massive outbreak in Europe.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.810
There was kind of no way
that it wasn't here already.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.470
We just weren't looking.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.030
So it was really not surprising at all.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.973
And we found one within the
first 400 samples we ran.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.720
The results of the sequencing
that labs like Lea's get,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.730
go into bigger systems that then feed

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.750
into a program called Nextstrain

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.700
that was created by Trevor Bedford.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.590
So tell us what we're looking at here.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:49.423
Oh boy.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.110
So here we can see the
viruses that were collected

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.390
over the course of the pandemic.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:57.900
Trevor's work focuses
on where mutations are

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.600
and how they spread around the world.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.470
And it's work that he's
done for other diseases,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.050
particularly flu.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.770
And that's part of the work that gets done

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.950
to like help build the new flu shots

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.490
and also work that's been
adapted to tuberculosis,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.610
Ebola, even Zika.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.810
So it's the kind of genomic
and scientific sequencing data

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.570
taken to the next level and
shown in that real world,

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:21.820
here's where the threats are,

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.430
here's where the virus
is emerging kind of way.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:26.740
So there's many countries,

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.570
many geographies that
didn't have any sequencing,

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.810
and now they're up sequencing thousands

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.890
of viruses every month.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.713
And this is a huge step into the future.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.780
How this whole thing works,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.580
how this genomic surveillance works

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.870
is that right now people will
get tested for coronarirus.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.870
Each of those swabs will result
in a 30,000 letter genome.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.170
And so each of the samples will have

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.860
slightly different mutations
in those 30,000 letters.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.890
And you use that to look for
variants that are emerging

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.870
and how they're spreading
and kind of learn a lot

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.363
about both the evolution and
epidemiology of the virus.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.130
So this is viruses that
are currently circulating,

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.360
that were collected from
the very early pandemic.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.520
So this is going to be
Wuhan in January, 2020.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.110
The first sequencing was really helpful

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.010
to establish human-to-human spread.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.700
So this was at a time
when China was downplaying

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.140
human-to-human spread,
but Chinese scientists

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.570
were openly sharing their sequencing data.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.010
And after that point, the
sequencing was really helpful

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.130
to understand how it was
spreading around the world.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.950
And then subsequent,
it's been really helpful

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.010
to understand variants and their spread

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.700
and whether one virus
is more transmissible

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.770
than another virus.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.780
Basically, if we can get
the whole world to be having

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:46.760
the ability to detect and sequence Covid,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.480
it would give us a headstart in terms

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.940
of when the next pandemic is emerging

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.683
as it could emerge from anywhere.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.180
My intentions are,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.990
and to the extent that I could
have any influence on it,

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:03.410
there will be a broad,
comprehensive approach

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:07.670
to pandemic preparedness
and response at every level.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.470
I mean, there have been calls recently

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.830
for increased pandemic preparedness

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.350
and building more capacity.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.210
One might imagine in
a future where there's

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.920
a lot of great respiratory
disease surveillance,

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.900
people having a swab in their home.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.960
And when they got that cough
they would swab themselves

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.480
and then send that back to the lab

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.300
and they could test
for multiple pathogens,

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.360
that would make a big difference.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.270
My goal is to try to make this
stuff a heck of a lot cheaper

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.970
so that we can kind of perform
this sort of surveillance

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.310
without breaking the bank
like we've been doing

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.850
for the last couple of years.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.420
I'm just worried that before
we can build something

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.610
like that on a national scale,

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.550
the political world will evaporate

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.850
and the appropriations
will evaporate with it.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.320
It's the work that's going on
when there's not a pandemic

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.080
that really prepares us for pandemics.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.488
And that probably sounds fairly obvious,

00:06:59.488 --> 00:07:01.900
but it's really important
to stop and realize

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.290
that we don't always fund that research.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.440
For us to be really prepared
and building better vaccines

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.890
that protect us against
threats that exist,

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:12.080
we really need to be
paying for basic research

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.210
to be going on all the time.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.280
Without this kind of funding,

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.560
so much amazing scientific
work could sit on shelf.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.860
That's really unfortunate.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.410
Scientists in South Africa

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.220
are warning of a new strain of Covid-19.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.130
The variant, which is yet to be named

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.120
appears to have a high
number of mutations.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.090
In the weeks after going to
Seattle, omicron emerged.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.690
And it's remarkable when
you think about the speed

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.913
at which the information about
omicron was made available.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.150
Had we not been sequencing

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.220
and had we not discovered omicron,

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.060
we would have just experienced a surge

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.090
in Covid cases with no reason.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.760
And we would have had no
understanding about that.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.540
And the reason some of this is possible

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.603
is because of private money
actually in public health.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.860
Hi Rick.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.190
There you are.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.800
Welcome to us.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.220
Rick Bright is a doctor
who's worked in government

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.010
and several different positions.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.410
He was a whistleblower under
the Trump administration.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:13.170
He came out publicly to air his concerns

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.640
about how the Trump administration

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.880
was handling pandemic preparedness.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:18.930
Our economy is spiraling downward,

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.970
and our population is
being paralyzed by fear

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:25.060
stemming from a lack of
a coordinated response

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.100
and a dearth of accurate,
clear communication

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.600
about the path forward.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:30.650
But he since rejoined

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.270
the Biden Covid transition team,

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.940
and now is part of the
Rockefeller Foundation

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.070
and he's heading up their
pandemic preparedness institute.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.730
I think governments move slowly.

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.210
They want to check and double check,

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.570
they want to evaluate things

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.830
and they wanna be absolutely certain

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.650
before they pull those triggers.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.390
And I saw that with the MERS outbreak,

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:56.410
with the H1N1 influenza
outbreak, with the Zika outbreak,

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.270
we've seen this repeatedly.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.620
We need to have entities in place

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.180
and organizations in place in our country

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.340
that can be less risk averse,

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.680
that can be more nimble and flexible

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.900
so the world can know what's happening,

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:13.880
and scientists and the public can be able

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.100
to better respond to those
newly emerging variants.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.090
Do we have enough sequencing

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.940
and is it rapid enough for us to be able

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:23.770
to enact public health measures,

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:26.570
to really stem the tide of another wave

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.330
of serious infections?

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.820
Genomic surveillance alone can't do much,

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.750
but genomic surveillance
and sequencing data

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:39.750
is a powerful dataset that we
can use to target resources

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.530
to areas where we know there
will be a surge in virus

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.420
to bolster the hospital
systems to make sure

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.360
there are sufficient medical
supplies in those areas

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.040
where we might see that virus emerging.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.520
Genomic surveillance is a
powerful leading indicator

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.280
that will allow us to target
our medical countermeasures

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.760
and our healthcare response.

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.470
The Biden administration
announced $1.7 billion

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:07.310
for improved sequencing, data sharing.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:08.460
Is it enough money?

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.560
Do we need more money to really
build the kind of systems

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.440
that we would say really
have put the safeguards

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:15.719
in place that we need?

00:10:15.719 --> 00:10:17.530
It was a lot of money.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.660
I don't think the money
has been distributed fully,

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.740
I don't think it has been
distributed fast enough.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.520
And we will be fighting this pandemic

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.490
in the rear view mirror
until we get in front

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.510
of a solid, robust surveillance system

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.850
that will also target vaccine breakthrough

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.530
and reinfection cases.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.600
Our vaccines are amazing,

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.830
they're wonderful and
they're doing a wonderful job

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.520
until they don't.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.980
And it's in those few
cases when they don't

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:48.040
where we really need to harness

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.873
that genomic surveillance
capability to ask why

00:10:51.873 --> 00:10:53.484
We've made a lot of progress.

00:10:53.484 --> 00:10:56.020
Don't discount that at all.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:57.790
My biggest concern though, Cynthia,

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:01.250
is all of these things that we've learned,

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:04.470
even the things that we've
done to make improvements

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.690
could just as easily be forgotten

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.130
the day after this pandemic ends.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:13.220
We need to make sure that
we're taking every step

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:17.410
to dig in and implement these new things

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:20.020
that we're learning for a new normal

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.953
going forward beyond the pandemic.

00:11:25.447 --> 00:11:27.680
Are we more prepared, yes and no.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.050
I think we know what we need to do,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.812
and that's half the battle at least.

00:11:32.812 --> 00:11:36.880
We know that we need to
be stockpiling supplies

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:38.520
to build tests.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.510
We know that testing is really important.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:44.500
We know that surveillance
is really important,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:49.497
but I am not sure that we're
going to be able to do that

00:11:49.497 --> 00:11:52.280
in a nimble fashion, but
I think science is ready.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.160
There are reasons to be
optimistic when you think

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.850
about pandemic preparedness now,

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.620
as compared to two years ago.

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:02.380
I think we do have a lot
more sequencing capability

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.390
in the U.S. and in the
world than we did previously

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:08.660
and a lot more of the
scientific work is getting done.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.500
But probably what's
critically missing here

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:14.390
is the sustainability of funding.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:17.070
And I think the other thing
that we really need to consider

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.890
and make sure that is
constantly in this conversation

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.510
is the benefits of sequencing
are tailoring the vaccine

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.510
to make sure that it's more effective.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.310
So we need sequencing going
on all over the world,

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.170
but we need the benefits
of that sequencing

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:33.050
to also flow through to the
people who are providing

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.060
that scientific information.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.170
That those countries are
also getting the vaccine

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.370
and making sure that it really
benefits the whole world,

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:42.153
not just a small sliver.

